<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Technology in Education</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> When It's Better to Ignore Technology</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Summarize the main points of different types of texts. Reading: Identify the main topic and related ideas in a simple structured text.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can summarize and compare key points in two texts about technology in school (e.g., "One article says that using tablets helps students because they can find information quickly, while the other says it's distracting because students use them to play games."). Can identify the main topic and supporting ideas in a text about educational apps (e.g., "The main idea is that some apps can help students memorize information faster."). Can summarize articles on classroom technology use preferences (e.g., "Two reasons students dislike technology in the classroom include the lack of charging stations and poor internet connections.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different scenarios of technology use in school (e.g., students using tablets for research, students playing games on phones during class, students collaborating on a digital project, students ignoring phones during group work). Students quickly state whether they *prefer* using or ignoring technology in each specific situation. They use simple present: "I prefer using technology for research" or "I think students *should* ignore phones during class."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to express preferences and give suggestions using target grammar and vocabulary. For instance, the teacher reads "Technology is a *useful* *device* for *education*, *but* sometimes it creates a *problem*," emphasizing stress in "ED-u-ca-tion" and intonation for contrast. The teacher also models how to use "should" for suggestions: "We *should* sometimes *limit* our use of screens."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary from the previous lesson and introduces words needed for the reading.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is a 'benefit,' is it a positive or a negative thing?" (Expected answer: "A positive thing.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I 'ignore' my phone, what am I doing with it?" (Expected answer: "Not paying attention to it.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology in Education".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Two short opinion articles from a school blog or magazine, titled "Tech: A Classroom Game Changer" (Text A) and "When to Power Down: Smart Tech Habits" (Text B). Both texts are simplified for A2.4 level, with clear main ideas and supporting details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets and understands information presented in various simple structured texts to summarize and extract main ideas.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activities:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive handouts with Text A and Text B.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pre-reading:</b> The teacher asks students to look at the titles and pictures (if any) and predict what each text will be about. "What do you think 'Tech: A Classroom Game Changer' means?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read both texts quickly (2-3 minutes) to get the main idea. The teacher asks: "Which text talks about the good things about technology? Which text talks about when not to use technology?" (Comprehension question 1)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Reading:</b> Students read the texts again, individually.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Comprehension:</b> The teacher presents 4-5 True/False statements based on the texts. For example: "Text A says technology makes *access* to *resources* easier." "Text B says we *should* *ignore* our *devices* during group discussions." (Comprehension questions 2-6)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying main topics and supporting ideas, using simple present for opinions, modal "should" for suggestions, and target vocabulary (technology, apps, tools, school, education, benefit, problem, solution, device, innovation, access, platform, software, curriculum, resources, system, use, ignore, prefer, benefit, advise, solve, engage, incorporate, limit, recommend, appropriate, useful, beneficial, innovative, educational, accessible, digital, technological, while, but, instead, because).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Gap-fill):</b> Students complete sentences taken directly from or inspired by the texts, using specific vocabulary words from a provided box (e.g., 'benefit', 'problem', 'ignore', 'access', 'solution', 'appropriate'). Example: "One big _______ of tablets is quick _______ to information." "Sometimes, it's _______ to _______ our phones and talk."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences about the texts using their own words, focusing on summarizing main ideas and expressing opinions/suggestions.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The main idea of Text A is that technology __________."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Text B suggests that we _________ technology when _________."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I think technology _________ (is/isn't) always _________ (beneficial/distracting) because _________."</li>
                            </ul>
                            The teacher emphasizes correct pronunciation of words like "beneficial" and "educational" and the intonation of "should" and "can" while students read their sentences.
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Check):</b> Students work in pairs to compare their completed sentences from Activity 2. They discuss any differences and justify their answers by referring back to the texts. The teacher monitors, listening for correct use of grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology in Education."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Summary and Comparison. Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to create a concise written summary that compares the main points of both texts. They will then orally present their summary to another group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Group Work:</b> In their groups, students reread both articles ("Tech: A Classroom Game Changer" and "When to Power Down: Smart Tech Habits"). They identify the main topic and supporting ideas for each text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Drafting Summary:</b> Each group collaboratively writes a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) that summarizes and compares the key points. They are encouraged to use phrases like "One article says...", "while the other argues...", "Text A highlights the *benefits* of *digital resources*, *but* Text B states we *should* sometimes *ignore* our *devices*." They must incorporate at least 5 vocabulary words from the list (e.g., benefit, problem, ignore, access, appropriate, solution) and use "should" or "can" at least once.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Presentation & Feedback:</b> Each group exchanges their written summary with another group. They read each other's summaries and verbally provide constructive feedback, checking if the main ideas are clear and if the comparison is accurate. They can ask Wh-questions like: "What *apps* does Text A *recommend*?" or "When *should* students *limit* their *device* use according to Text B?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and peer presentation, focusing on students' ability to summarize and compare information from the texts, and their use of target language. A simple checklist can be used.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher collects the written group summaries. Additionally, during the peer presentation, the teacher listens to groups as they articulate their summaries and provide feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Summary:</b> Clearly identifies main points of both texts.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comparison:</b> Effectively compares and contrasts ideas from the two texts using discourse markers (while, but, instead, because).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Target Language Use:</b> Incorporates at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., benefit, problem, ignore, access, appropriate) and uses modal "should" or "can" correctly.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Coherence:</b> Summary is well-organized and easy to understand.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can identify the main topic in both texts: Yes / Partially / No"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can summarize the key points of the texts: Yes / Partially / No"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today was __________."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading and summarizing the articles did you find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can identifying the main ideas and supporting details help you when you read other subjects like history or science?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The group summaries effectively demonstrated students' ability to extract and compare main ideas, particularly when using discourse markers like 'while' and 'but'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students struggled with consistently incorporating the modal verb 'should' for suggestions; more explicit modeling might be needed for the next lesson.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adjustments for next lesson: Ensure clearer examples of how to link ideas with 'because' and 'instead' when expressing opinions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great work summarizing and comparing the texts today! In our next lesson, we will use these ideas to have a full class discussion and debate about when we *should* or *should not* use technology in school. Be ready to share your opinions!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>